WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.564
The phone rings at the office front desk.

2
00:00:05.564 --> 00:00:11.012
A customer's order failed this morning, and they read you a reference: 7f3a9c.

3
00:00:11.233 --> 00:00:15.207
Four departments each keep a logbook, a few thousand lines long.

4
00:00:15.207 --> 00:00:18.436
The answer is in there, and the customer is waiting.

5
00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:21.232
Nobody reads four logbooks from the top.

6
00:00:21.232 --> 00:00:22.899
You ask questions instead.

7
00:00:22.899 --> 00:00:28.415
But here's one nobody asks: when your search prints nothing at all, is that good news?

8
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:30.004
Hold on to that one.

9
00:00:30.004 --> 00:00:33.020
By the end, you'll know why silence can lie.

10
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:36.954
Every department keeps a logbook at its door.

11
00:00:36.954 --> 00:00:42.849
A log file is the same idea: a plain text file a program adds to, one line per event.

12
00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:47.439
Here is one page of the lab's app.log, which has 37 lines.

13
00:00:47.439 --> 00:00:54.601
Each line holds a time, a level like INFO or ERROR, a service name, a request ID and a message.

14
00:00:54.601 --> 00:00:56.335
We trimmed them to fit.

15
00:00:56.567 --> 00:01:02.352
Picture running a highlighter down every line of the page, then keeping only the marked lines.

16
00:01:02.352 --> 00:01:04.260
That is exactly what grep does.

17
00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:12.324
grep reads its input one line at a time, checks whether the pattern appears anywhere in the line, and keeps the lines where it does.

18
00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:15.551
Four lines: the customer's request on this page.

19
00:01:15.551 --> 00:01:21.272
The highlighter is the pattern, the page is the input, and the marked lines are the output.

20
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.942
Each option answers a question you'll ask when something breaks.

21
00:01:25.942 --> 00:01:28.221
Start plain: grep ERROR, in capitals.

22
00:01:28.433 --> 00:01:30.601
In the whole file, that is 4 lines.

23
00:01:30.601 --> 00:01:32.521
You can see three of them here.

24
00:01:32.733 --> 00:01:33.842
Did I miss any?

25
00:01:33.842 --> 00:01:37.390
Logs are messy about case, so dash I ignores it.

26
00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:38.203
Five lines.

27
00:01:38.203 --> 00:01:42.637
The extra one is a WARN line that says retrying after error.

28
00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:45.349
How many lines say 200?

29
00:01:45.349 --> 00:01:47.831
Dash C counts them: 16.

30
00:01:47.831 --> 00:01:49.882
But look at line 6.

31
00:01:49.882 --> 00:01:52.472
1200ms contains 200 too.

32
00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:56.734
Add dash W, and only 200 standing alone counts.

33
00:01:56.734 --> 00:01:58.708
The answer drops to 14.

34
00:01:59.433 --> 00:02:04.770
grep also hands back a number when it ends, called its exit code.

35
00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.643
The shell keeps the last one in $?.

36
00:02:07.643 --> 00:02:10.106
After a quiet grep -q, echo $?

37
00:02:10.106 --> 00:02:10.845
prints 0.

38
00:02:11.067 --> 00:02:12.224
Zero means found.

39
00:02:12.224 --> 00:02:13.518
One means no match.

40
00:02:13.518 --> 00:02:17.060
Two means something went wrong, like a missing file.

41
00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:19.763
Now a harder question.

42
00:02:19.763 --> 00:02:25.005
You want every duration on the page: 18ms, 1200ms, 3ms.

43
00:02:25.005 --> 00:02:28.055
No single word matches them all.

44
00:02:28.267 --> 00:02:31.241
But they share a shape: some digits, then ms.

45
00:02:31.241 --> 00:02:36.792
A pattern that describes a shape like that is called a regular expression, or regex.

46
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.664
Square brackets with zero to nine means any one digit.

47
00:02:40.664 --> 00:02:42.767
A plus means one or more of it.

48
00:02:42.767 --> 00:02:44.124
Then the letters ms.

49
00:02:44.333 --> 00:02:49.764
Run it with -o, which prints only the matched part, and -E, more on that soon.

50
00:02:49.764 --> 00:02:51.365
Out come the durations.

51
00:02:51.567 --> 00:02:52.880
A few more symbols.

52
00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:54.747
A dot is any one character.

53
00:02:54.747 --> 00:02:57.928
A star is zero or more of the thing before it.

54
00:02:57.928 --> 00:03:01.593
Caret and dollar pin the start and the end of a line.

55
00:03:01.593 --> 00:03:02.907
And a bar means or.

56
00:03:03.133 --> 00:03:06.072
So you want every line with ERROR or WARN.

57
00:03:06.072 --> 00:03:09.220
You type grep, then ERROR bar WARN in quotes.

58
00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:10.619
No dash E this time.

59
00:03:10.619 --> 00:03:11.459
What prints?

60
00:03:14.667 --> 00:03:15.378
Nothing.

61
00:03:15.378 --> 00:03:16.533
Not one line.

62
00:03:16.533 --> 00:03:19.465
And the exit code is 1: no match.

63
00:03:19.667 --> 00:03:20.542
Here's why.

64
00:03:20.542 --> 00:03:27.785
Plain grep speaks an older, basic dialect of regex, where the bar is an ordinary character.

65
00:03:27.785 --> 00:03:32.401
So grep looked for the exact text E R R O R, bar, W A R N.

66
00:03:32.633 --> 00:03:35.691
And not a single line in this logbook contains that.

67
00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:40.032
Add dash E, for extended regex, where the bar means or.

68
00:03:40.032 --> 00:03:41.609
Now it finds 8 lines.

69
00:03:41.833 --> 00:03:43.687
So the first grep did answer.

70
00:03:43.687 --> 00:03:47.393
It answered, perfectly, a question you never meant to ask.

71
00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.215
That's our open question from the start.

72
00:03:50.215 --> 00:03:53.876
An empty result answers your pattern, not your question.

73
00:03:53.876 --> 00:03:57.145
So check the pattern before you trust the silence.

74
00:03:57.145 --> 00:03:57.864
And echo $?

75
00:03:57.864 --> 00:04:01.003
tells you whether grep found nothing, or failed.

76
00:04:02.333 --> 00:04:03.325
One more trap.

77
00:04:03.325 --> 00:04:06.869
Last time, the clerk read a star as any file name.

78
00:04:06.869 --> 00:04:11.192
Inside grep, a star means zero or more of the last character.

79
00:04:11.192 --> 00:04:13.176
Same symbols, two languages.

80
00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:16.615
In this one command, both live side by side.

81
00:04:16.615 --> 00:04:20.854
The clerk expands the unquoted logs/*.log into file names.

82
00:04:20.854 --> 00:04:25.239
It leaves the regex alone, because it sits in single quotes.

83
00:04:25.467 --> 00:04:27.737
So the quotes decide who reads what.

84
00:04:27.737 --> 00:04:30.259
Put every grep pattern in single quotes.

85
00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:35.586
And if you have used regex in a programming language, leave backslash D at home.

86
00:04:35.586 --> 00:04:37.762
GNU grep reads it as the letter d.

87
00:04:37.762 --> 00:04:40.257
Write zero to nine in brackets instead.

88
00:04:40.967 --> 00:04:42.657
So, the moral of the story.

89
00:04:42.657 --> 00:04:46.600
grep is a highlighter: it keeps the lines that match a pattern.

90
00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:48.853
A regex describes text by its shape.

91
00:04:48.853 --> 00:04:51.419
And silence is an answer to your pattern.

92
00:04:51.419 --> 00:04:54.611
Check it, before you tell the customer all is well.

93
00:04:55.333 --> 00:04:56.387
A quick check.

94
00:04:56.387 --> 00:05:00.829
Which command counts the lines of app.log that end with ms?

95
00:05:04.033 --> 00:05:04.934
The third one.

96
00:05:04.934 --> 00:05:07.958
Dollar pins the pattern to the end of the line.

97
00:05:07.958 --> 00:05:11.755
With dash F, grep would hunt for the text ms dollar itself.
